WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.670
Vision-language navigation policies need a
demonstration for every new route.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.750
Cut the demonstrations by 30%, and success
drops by nearly 20 points.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:15.861
Yet each demonstration already shows the
future: which landmark comes next,

00:00:15.861 --> 00:00:18.600
what it looks like, and its 3D structure.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:23.025
FINE turns this future into a teacher,
used only during training.

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:25.202
First: what should appear next?

00:00:25.202 --> 00:00:29.757
Explicit landmark tokens follow the
instruction's ordered landmarks,

00:00:29.757 --> 00:00:35.250
and predict the future landmark region in
semantic and 3D geometric feature space.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.561
Second: which future is consistent with
the route?

00:00:38.561 --> 00:00:43.055
An implicit future token learns the future
the route actually reaches,

00:00:43.055 --> 00:00:47.550
against same-scene hard negatives from a
fine-tuned video world model.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:52.656
At deployment, these branches are dropped:
no future frames, no rollouts,

00:00:52.656 --> 00:00:55.125
and only 5 milliseconds of overhead.

00:00:55.225 --> 00:01:01.975
FINE raises success by 2.6 points on
R2R-CE and 4.5 on RxR-CE.

00:01:01.975 --> 00:01:06.875
With 70% of the data, the gain is 6.8
points.

00:01:06.975 --> 00:01:12.574
On a real humanoid, gains are largest with
hidden landmarks: 30 success points,

00:01:12.574 --> 00:01:14.700
and 20 on room-to-room routes.
